# When was the first text message sent?
subtitle: "Guess the year of the first text message, then hear what it said, who got it, and why he could not reply."

## When was the first text message sent?
affirm: playful
text: {"font_size":32}
sources:
    - id: voda
      title: 25 years since the world's first text message
      authors: Vodafone
      year: 2017
      url: https://www.vodafone.com/news/newsroom/technology/25-anniversary-text-message
      finding: Neil Papworth sent 'Merry Christmas' from a computer to Richard Jarvis's Orbitel 901 on 3 December 1992
    - id: hill
      title: SMS
      authors: Wikipedia
      year: 2025
      url: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/SMS
      finding: Friedhelm Hillebrand set the 160-character limit in 1985 after counting the characters in typical postcards and telex messages
    - id: nft
      title: First SMS sells as NFT for 107,000 euros
      authors: Reuters
      year: 2021
      finding: Vodafone auctioned the 'Merry Christmas' text as an NFT in Paris, December 2021

    scale year cites ["voda"] x 150 y 520 width 700 min 1970 max 2010 value 1992 ticks 4 hidden true
    box bubble "Merry Christmas" #2f6b8f x 500 y 320 width 330 font_size 40 hidden true
    text limit "1985: 160 characters" #b5482e cites ["hill"] x 380 y 620 font_size 28 hidden true
    icon pc size 150 set twemoji x 200 y 320 icon_look picture of "desktop computer" hidden true
    icon phone size 150 set twemoji x 800 y 320 icon_look picture of "mobile phone" hidden true
    text noreply "no reply" #b5482e x 800 y 205 font_size 34 hidden true
    path mark85 thick #b5482e points [[412.5,525],[412.5,595]] hidden true

    card title "When was the first text sent?"

Billions of text messages are sent every day. But when was the very first one sent?
    draw year

    ask "In what year was the first text message sent? Click on the line." on year store y default "1998" tolerance 0.05
        right: Close. You know your phones.
        wrong: Not quite. It came before most people owned a mobile.

On the third of December 1992, a twenty-two-year-old engineer, Neil Papworth, sent two words: Merry Christmas.
    draw pc bubble

He typed it on a computer, because no phone could write a message yet.
    highlight pc

It reached Richard Jarvis of Vodafone, at his office Christmas party.
    draw phone

His phone could receive it, but he had no way to reply.
    draw noreply

The famous limit of one hundred and sixty characters is older still.
    draw mark85 limit

In 1985, Friedhelm Hillebrand counted the letters on postcards and telex messages, and found that was enough to say most things.
    highlight limit

In 2021 Vodafone sold that first message as a digital collectible, for a hundred and seven thousand euros.
    highlight bubble

Not bad for a Christmas greeting that could not be answered.
    point at.ref bubble gesture underline
